## In Plain Words

This summary helps you read the terms below. It is not part of them, and where they differ the terms win.

What you can do:

1. Use Typster for free for your own personal purposes: study, hobby, a thesis, your own documents. That stays free even if you later sell or publish what you wrote.
2. Use Typster for free inside a registered nonprofit or charity, for its nonprofit mission.
3. Use the official Typster website. Its own terms of service govern that, not this license.
4. Read, change and share the code under these terms.
5. Keep everything you make. Your documents, templates and PDFs are yours.
6. Count on no rug pull. Copies already released keep these terms, and if the project is abandoned for 24 months the latest version also becomes available under the AGPL-3.0.

What you can't do:

1. Use Typster in any business, or in any school, university, government body or other organization that isn't a registered nonprofit or charity, without a paid commercial license from the licensor. That includes internal use inside a company.
2. Use Typster's code, documentation or design to build a competing product or service, even a free or noncommercial one.
3. Keep your changes private once you share them or let others use them over a network. You must publish them under these same terms.
4. Remove the credit to Typster and its author.
5. Use the Typster name or logo to promote your own product without permission.

## Terms

### 1. Acceptance

In order to get any license under these terms, you must agree to them as both strict obligations and conditions to all your licenses. By copying, downloading, cloning, running or changing the software, you agree to these terms.

### 2. Copyright License

The licensor grants you a copyright license for the software to do everything you might do with the software that would otherwise infringe the licensor's copyright in it for any permitted purpose. However, you may only distribute the software according to [Distribution License](#3-distribution-license) and make changes or new works based on the software according to [Changes and Share-Alike](#5-changes-and-share-alike).

### 3. Distribution License

The licensor grants you an additional copyright license to distribute copies of the software for any permitted purpose. Your license to distribute covers distributing the software with changes and new works permitted by [Changes and Share-Alike](#5-changes-and-share-alike), but only under these terms, with the notices required by [Notices and Attribution](#4-notices-and-attribution), and with the Corresponding Source included or offered as required by [Source Availability and Publication](#6-source-availability-and-publication).

### 4. Notices and Attribution

(a) You must ensure that anyone who gets a copy of any part of the software from you also gets a copy of these terms, as well as copies of any plain-text lines beginning with `Required Notice:` that the licensor provided with the software, and any `NOTICE` file the licensor provided with it.

(b) Every file you change must carry a prominent notice saying that you changed it and the date of the change.

(c) Every running copy of the software that anyone other than you can use, changed or not, must show all of its users, from its main interface and reachable from the editor itself, the line "Based on Typster by Henri (Anrie) Abrahamyan", a link to these terms, and a link to the Corresponding Source of the version that is running. A footer or an "About" page reachable from the editor is enough. A notice only on a landing page, or one removed from a compiled or minified bundle, is not.

(d) You may not remove, hide or change any copyright notice, `Required Notice:` line, or attribution the licensor provided with the software.

### 5. Changes and Share-Alike

(a) The licensor grants you an additional copyright license to make changes and new works based on the software for any permitted purpose.

(b) If you distribute a work based on the software, or let anyone other than you use it over a network, you must license that whole work to everyone under these same terms, without adding any terms, unless you have a separate written license from the licensor that says otherwise.

(c) A separate program that only communicates with the software at arm's length, such as over a documented network interface or command line, and that contains no part of the software, is not a work based on the software just because of that communication.

### 6. Source Availability and Publication

(a) **Network use.** If anyone other than you uses a work based on the software over a network, that work must offer every such user, prominently and at no charge, a way to get its Corresponding Source from a network server.

(b) **Distribution.** If you distribute the software or a work based on it in any form other than source code, you must include its Corresponding Source, or a written offer, valid for at least three years, to provide it at no more than the cost of providing it.

(c) **Publication.** Within 30 days after you first distribute a work based on the software, or first let anyone other than you use it over a network, you must publish its Corresponding Source in a public repository that anyone can reach without payment or registration, under these terms.

(d) **Private changes.** A work based on the software that you never distribute and that nobody but you uses does not have to be published.

(e) **What you may keep back.** You do not have to publish passwords, keys, credentials, deployment configuration specific to your own servers, or your users' data.

### 7. Patent License

The licensor grants you a patent license for the software that covers patent claims the licensor can license, or becomes able to license, that you would infringe by using the software for any permitted purpose.

### 8. Permitted Purposes

Only the uses in this section are permitted purposes. Any other use is commercial use. A use listed in [Commercial Use](#9-commercial-use) is commercial use even if it also fits 8.1 or 8.2. Section 8.3 applies despite section 9(b)(1).

#### 8.1 Personal Use

Use by an individual for their own personal purposes, such as personal study, research, experiment, hobby projects, private entertainment, and writing their own documents, is use for a permitted purpose, even if those documents are later sold or published, as long as writing them is not part of a business, trade or profession. Use on behalf of an employer or a client, or in the course of any business, trade or profession, is not personal use.

#### 8.2 Nonprofit Organizations

Use by a registered charity or registered nonprofit organization, by its own staff and volunteers, for its nonprofit mission, is use for a permitted purpose regardless of the source of its funding. Offering the software, or a service built on it, to the public for a fee or other payment is not covered by this section. Nor is an organization under the control of a for-profit organization, or use for the benefit of for-profit organizations.

#### 8.3 Contributions Back

Running, building and testing the software only to prepare a contribution submitted to the licensor's official repository under the licensor's contributor license agreement is use for a permitted purpose, whoever the contributor works for. This does not cover any other use, including production use.

### 9. Commercial Use

(a) Commercial use requires a separate written commercial license from the licensor. These terms grant no license for it, for any period, including evaluation or trial.

(b) Commercial use includes, without limitation:

1. any use by or for a for-profit organization, sole proprietorship, freelancer or self-employed person in the course of business, including internal use by its own staff with no resale;
2. offering the software, or a service built on it, to others for a fee, subscription, advertising or any other payment, or hosting it for others as part of a business;
3. installing, operating, customizing or supporting the software for a client for a fee;
4. including the software in a product that is sold or licensed for a fee.

(c) A commercial license may allow changes to be kept private and may waive other conditions of these terms. Only the licensor can grant one.

### 10. No Competing Products

(a) You may not use the software, any part of it, or any work based on it, to build, offer or operate a competing product, even for a permitted purpose and even at no charge, unless you have a separate written license from the licensor that allows it.

(b) You may not use the source code, documentation or design of the software as a reference, model or specification to build a competing product.

(c) A **competing product** is any product or service, paid or free, that is a substitute for the software or for any product or service the licensor offers based on it, such as a hosted or self-hosted editor for writing, compiling, previewing or collaborating on Typst documents, or any feature the licensor sells.

(d) Running the software or a work based on it for your own permitted purpose, and publishing changes as [Changes and Share-Alike](#5-changes-and-share-alike) and [Source Availability and Publication](#6-source-availability-and-publication) require, is not building, offering or operating a competing product. Making it available to the general public, or promoting it as an alternative to the software or the licensor's services, is.

### 11. Your Content

Documents, Typst source files, templates, packages, fonts, images, PDFs and any other content that you or your users write, import or produce with the software, other than the software or any part of it, are not the software and are not works based on it. These terms place no conditions on them. Snippets and boilerplate that the software itself inserts into your files are licensed to you without conditions.

### 12. Official Typster Service

These terms cover copies of the software. Using the official Typster website and services operated by the licensor is governed by their own terms of service, not by these terms, and is available to everyone on those terms.

### 13. No Further Restrictions

You may not impose any further terms, fees or technical measures that limit anyone's exercise of the rights these terms grant. Each recipient of the software, or of a work based on it, gets a license directly from the licensor under these terms.

### 14. No Surrender

If a court order, an agreement or anything else imposes conditions on you that conflict with these terms, they do not excuse you from these terms. If you cannot meet both, you may not distribute the software, or let others use it over a network, at all.

### 15. Fair Use

You may have "fair use" rights for the software under the law. These terms do not limit them.

### 16. No Other Rights

These terms do not allow you to sublicense or transfer any of your licenses to anyone else, or prevent the licensor from granting licenses to anyone else. These terms do not imply any other licenses. They grant no rights in the name Typster or its logos, except to state the origin of the software as required by [Notices and Attribution](#4-notices-and-attribution). You may not use them to promote your product or service without the licensor's written permission.

### 17. Patent Defense

If you make any written claim that the software infringes or contributes to infringement of any patent, your patent license for the software granted under these terms ends immediately. If your company makes such a claim, your patent license ends immediately for work on behalf of your company.

### 18. Exclusions

These terms cover only the parts of the software whose copyright the licensor holds or controls. Third-party components keep their own licenses, as listed in their files or in the `NOTICE` file, including the Typst compiler and its web bundles, fonts, and third-party icons and brand marks. The private `vendor/pro` component is proprietary and is not licensed under these terms.

### 19. Violations

(a) The first time you are notified in writing that you have violated any of these terms, or done anything with the software not covered by your licenses, your licenses can nonetheless continue if you come into full compliance with these terms, and take practical steps to correct past violations, within 32 days of receiving notice. Otherwise, all your licenses end immediately.

(b) Coming into compliance does not license any commercial use you already made. That use stays unlicensed unless the licensor grants you a commercial license covering it.

(c) If you violate these terms again after a notice, all your licenses end immediately, with no further chance to cure.

(d) The licenses of people who received the software from you and comply with these terms do not end because yours did.

### 20. No Liability

***As far as the law allows, the software comes as is, without any warranty or condition, and the licensor will not be liable to you for any damages arising out of these terms or the use or nature of the software, under any kind of legal claim.***

### 21. Severability and Language

If a court finds any part of these terms unenforceable, the rest stays in effect, and that part is enforced as far as the law allows. If these terms are translated, the English text controls.

### 22. No Rug Pull

(a) The licensor will not withdraw these terms from a copy of the software it has already made available. Your licenses for that copy end only as described in [Violations](#19-violations) and [Patent Defense](#17-patent-defense).

(b) The licensor grants you now, taking effect only on the Abandonment Date, an additional license to use the latest version of the software published by the licensor on that date under the GNU Affero General Public License, version 3, only. Sections 8, 9 and 10 do not limit that additional license. [Exclusions](#18-exclusions) still applies to it.

(c) The **Abandonment Date** is the first day on which, for the previous 24 months in a row, the licensor has done none of the following: published a new commit or release of the software in its official repository or an announced successor repository, operated the official Typster website or services, or offered commercial licenses for the software.

### 23. Definitions

The **licensor** is the individual or entity named as licensor above, and the **software** is the software the licensor makes available under these terms.

**You** refers to the individual or entity agreeing to these terms. For a nonprofit organization allowed by [Nonprofit Organizations](#82-nonprofit-organizations), use by its own staff and volunteers counts as use by it throughout these terms.

**Your company** is any legal entity, sole proprietorship, or other kind of organization that you work for, plus all organizations that have control over, are under the control of, or are under common control with that organization. **Control** means ownership of substantially all the assets of an entity, or the power to direct its management and policies by vote, contract, or otherwise. Control can be direct or indirect. Anything you do with the software on behalf of your company counts as your company's use.

**Your licenses** are all the licenses granted to you for the software under these terms.

**Use** means anything you do with the software requiring one of your licenses.

A **work based on the software** is the software with changes, or any work that contains or is derived from the software or any part of it.

**Corresponding Source** of a work means all the source code needed to build, install, run and change that work, including build and installation scripts, interface definitions, and the source of any libraries or services the work is specifically designed to require, but not general-purpose system components, compilers or tools that are not part of the work.

**Use over a network** means letting anyone interact with the software, or a work based on it, remotely, such as through a website, an application programming interface, or an embedded editor.
